Board: Arduino Nano 33 BLE Sense, Arduino mbed core

<handout>
# Lab 5: Serial "student ID" responder (mock handout)

> Mock handout written for testing `grader make-plan` with per-student values. It is not
> an official course document.

## Goal

Write a sketch that answers simple text commands over a serial port, using values worked
out from **your own university ID**. Every student's correct answers are different.

## Hardware

* **Serial1** (pins D0/D1), not the USB `Serial` port, at **9600 baud**, 8N1.
* Nothing else is connected.

## Commands

The grader sends one command per line. A line may end with `\n` or with `\r\n`, and your
sketch must accept both (the `\r` is part of the line ending, not of the command). Ignore
empty lines.

| Command | Reply (one line) |
|---|---|
| `ID?` | your 9-digit university ID, e.g. `905123456` |
| `SUM?` | the sum of your ID's digits, e.g. `35` |
| `HEX?` | your ID as an upper-case hexadecimal number without leading zeros, e.g. `35F31680` |
| anything else | `ERR` |

End every reply with `\r\n` (`Serial1.println`) or `\n`.

## Requirements

1. Use your own university ID. Keep the leading zeros in the `ID?` reply.
2. Reply promptly to every command, for as long as the board is powered: the sketch must
   keep answering, not just the first time.
3. Print nothing except the replies (no start-up message, no debug output on Serial1).
4. Comment your code.

## Submission

Submit your sketch folder.
</handout>

<requirements>
[
  {
    "id": "serial1_config",
    "requirement": "The sketch configures Serial1 (pins D0/D1) for communication at 9600 baud, 8N1, instead of using the USB Serial port."
  },
  {
    "id": "id_reply",
    "requirement": "On receiving the command ID?, the board replies with the student's own 9-digit university ID, keeping any leading zeros."
  },
  {
    "id": "sum_reply",
    "requirement": "On receiving the command SUM?, the board replies with the sum of the digits of the student's ID."
  },
  {
    "id": "hex_reply",
    "requirement": "On receiving the command HEX?, the board replies with the student's ID converted to upper-case hexadecimal, with no leading zeros."
  },
  {
    "id": "unknown_command_err",
    "requirement": "On receiving any line that is not ID?, SUM?, or HEX?, the board replies with ERR."
  },
  {
    "id": "line_ending_tolerance",
    "requirement": "The board accepts command lines terminated by either \\n or \\r\\n, treating the \\r as part of the line ending rather than part of the command."
  },
  {
    "id": "ignore_empty_lines",
    "requirement": "The board ignores empty lines (lines containing only a line ending) and does not send any reply for them."
  },
  {
    "id": "prompt_reply",
    "requirement": "The board replies to each valid command within 100 ms of receiving the line ending."
  },
  {
    "id": "continuous_operation",
    "requirement": "The board keeps answering correctly to repeated commands for as long as it stays powered, not only the first time."
  },
  {
    "id": "reply_terminator",
    "requirement": "Every reply sent by the board ends with \\r\\n or \\n."
  },
  {
    "id": "no_extra_output",
    "requirement": "The board prints nothing on Serial1 other than the defined replies, with no start-up message or debug output."
  }
]
</requirements>

<student_values>
- student_id: <student_id>
- digit_sum: 23
- id_hex: DC895
</student_values>

<student_code untrusted="true">
=== answers_once.ino ===
   1| // Lab 5 - serial "student ID" responder
   2| //
   3| // Serial1 @ 9600. setup() waits for a command line, strips the line
   4| // ending with trim(), skips blank lines, and sends the matching reply.
   5| //
   6| // ID <student_id> -> digit sum 9+3+3+1+7 = 23, hex 903317 = DC895
   7| 
   8| const char ID[] = "<student_id>";
   9| 
  10| void sendReply(const String &cmd) {
  11|   if (cmd == "ID?") {
  12|     Serial1.println(ID);
  13|   } else if (cmd == "SUM?") {
  14|     Serial1.println(23);
  15|   } else if (cmd == "HEX?") {
  16|     Serial1.println("DC895");
  17|   } else {
  18|     Serial1.println("ERR");
  19|   }
  20| }
  21| 
  22| void setup() {
  23|   Serial1.begin(9600);
  24| 
  25|   String cmd = "";
  26|   // keep reading lines until we get a non-empty one
  27|   while (cmd.length() == 0) {
  28|     if (Serial1.available()) {
  29|       cmd = Serial1.readStringUntil('\n');
  30|       cmd.trim();
  31|     }
  32|   }
  33|   sendReply(cmd);
  34| }
  35| 
  36| void loop() {
  37| }
</student_code>
